/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  optim07_mads_mixed_column / system/outerDict

  WHAT THIS CASE IS: the column question of optim06 -- how many stages, at
  what reflux, to make the purity spec for the least reboiler vapour? --
  solved in ONE run by `method mads;` with the stage count `nStages`
  (integer) and the reflux ratio (continuous) searched TOGETHER:

      minimise V_strip  over  nStages in {8..14} (integer),  R in [1.5, 6]
      subject to x_D_LK >= 0.98

  optim06 declares nStages `integer;` and the driver ENUMERATES it: one full
  SQP per stage count, seven runs, every value on a table.  Here nothing is
  enumerated.  MADS (Audet & Dennis; solver/MADS.H says what was read) polls
  mesh points around the best point it has: the integer variable moves in
  whole stages (its mesh never goes below 1), the reflux in a mesh that
  shrinks as the search closes in, and the purity constraint rides on the
  PROGRESSIVE BARRIER -- an infeasible trial point is kept while its
  violation h is below a threshold that falls with the iterations, so the
  search may cross the spec on its way and is pushed back to it.  No
  gradient is taken anywhere, so a non-smooth response, a regime switch or a
  table lookup would not break it; that is its ground, and the one the SQP
  (smooth, shadow prices) and the enumeration (one integer) do not cover.

  WHAT A STUDENT SEES: every iteration prints the poll size Delta, the mesh
  size delta, how many points were polled and how many were already in the
  cache (a repeated point is not simulated twice), the outcome (dominating /
  improving / unsuccessful), the best objective, the violation h of the
  infeasible incumbent and the barrier threshold hMax; the end prints why
  it stopped, the NUMBER OF SIMULATIONS, and which constraints are active.
  Put the simulation count beside optim06's (its integer table prints the
  evaluations of all seven SQP runs).  MEASURED (docs/audits/2026-11-24-
  optimizer-comparison.md): the same optimum, nStages = 14 (the upper
  bound) at R = 2.90, V_strip 0.034821 against optim06's 0.034790 kmol/s
  (0.09 % above: MADS stops on its poll-size tolerance, tolX), in 126
  simulations against the enumeration's 74 -- MADS is NOT cheaper here, and
  the smooth SQP inside the enumeration is the better tool for this smooth
  problem.  What each promises: MADS, a local optimum on the mesh, no
  multipliers; the enumeration, an optimum for each stage count, all
  visible, and the refusals (N = 8, N = 9..11) on the table.  The case is
  here for what MADS does that the enumeration cannot: more than one
  integer variable, a non-smooth response, no inner solver at all.  It
  cost MORE simulations than the enumeration (126 against 74); what it buys
  instead is one run, mixed variables and no enumeration grammar -- not a
  better answer.

  Same seed (default 0), same run: the poll directions come from a Halton
  sequence indexed by the seed, so the output is reproducible.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

type    optimization;
method  mads;

variables
(
    {
        path     units[0].operation.nStages;
        integer  true;
        min      8;
        max      14;
        initial  11;
    }
    {
        path     units[0].operation.refluxRatio;
        min      1.5;
        max      6.0;
        initial  3.0;
    }
);

objective
{
    kind     kpi;
    path     column01.V_strip;
    sense    minimise;
}

constraints
(
    { kpi column01.x_D_LK;  atLeast 0.98; }
);

options
{
    maxEvaluations  300;
    tolX            1e-3;     // stop when the poll size is below 0.1 % of a range
    seed            0;
}
